# Limitations — both closed by the v2 iteration [DONE 2026-08-27]

- string escape [DONE]
  String literals now take `\n \t \r \\ \" \` \0` and `\u{...}`. The unicode
  escape is braced rather than fixed at four hex digits, so an astral code
  point needs no surrogate pair. Resolved by the parser into the literal it
  denotes, so an escape leaves no trace in the core AST.

  This is also where the port earned its keep: PureScript's `Char` is a
  UTF-16 code unit, so `fromCharCode` rejected everything above U+FFFF and
  every astral escape failed there while parsing fine in Haskell. Decoding to
  a `CodePoint` fixed it. Neither implementation's own suite could have found
  that.

- node in the computation block [DONE]
  Closed by the change the whole iteration turns on: documents are
  expressions. v1 had two disjoint ASTs — `Expr` for the computation block,
  `TemplateNode` for the template block — so a binding could not hold a
  document at all. `Element` and `Fragment` are now ordinary `Expr`
  constructors, and a binding holds one like any other value:

      @header=.header(.h1("`$ctx.title`"))
      @kids=.(.p("one"), .p("two"))
      @panel=(title, children) => .section(.h2($title), $children)
      .main($header, $panel("Deployment", $kids))

  That is what makes the JSX-children pattern work without a separate
  template-value category, and it deleted the entire template-phase AST along
  with its duplicated map/branch forms.

See specs/reference.md for the language as it now stands.
